    "Disassembly and cross references of a configured target's reference image.\n"
    "\n"
    "    disasm.exe <target> 0x00401000                 one function: up to the int3 padding after a ret / jmp, or\n"
    "                                                   the next known start (an annotated function, a symbol that\n"
    "                                                   something calls or points at); a line says so when the\n"
    "                                                   symbol source's size differs\n"
    "    disasm.exe <target> 0x00401000 --size 0x60     a fixed range\n"
    "    disasm.exe <target> 0x00401000 --xref          callers (call rel32), jumps into it (jmp rel32: tail jumps,\n"
    "                                                   thunks; jcc rel32) and absolute references (relocated words)\n"
    "    disasm.exe <target> 0x00402000 --data --words 12   a table: one dword per line, relocated words marked,\n"
    "                                                   each named (annotation, symbol, import, function+offset)\n"
    "    disasm.exe <target> --name REGEX               look symbol names up (case-insensitive, Python syntax)\n"
    "    disasm.exe <target> 0x00401000 --no-bytes      without the raw bytes\n"
    "    disasm.exe <target> --thunks [0x<start> --size 0x<n>] [--all]\n"
    "                                                   the jmp thunks (`jmp rel32`, `mov ecx, imm32; jmp rel32`) that\n"
    "                                                   a relocated word or a call points at and no symbol names:\n"
    "                                                   callbacks stored as data whose body is a jump; each with its\n"
    "                                                   target, the target's name and the first reference\n"
    "\n"
    "<target> is a target of config/ctools.json: its image (checked against the pinned sha256) and its symbol\n"
    "sources give the names and sizes; the units' annotations give known function starts and table labels.\n"
    "Numbers are hexadecimal.\n"
